{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"mushroomy-rightable-cricket","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"Follow-up from edeb-c3ad-c051-4e6a (closed duplicate; the active flake was fixed under e9d5/d46c332c by switching effect_spies from monkeypatch.setattr to patch.object).\n\nLATENT (not active) fragility observed during the edeb investigation: the autouse `_network_guard` fixture installs its socket-connect guard via `patch.object` and relies on strict-LIFO teardown. If a FUTURE test/fixture patches `socket.socket.connect`/`connect_ex` via a mechanism that is not LIFO-aware (e.g. monkeypatch.setattr, whose dict-snapshot restore blindly setattrs), its teardown can silently re-install `_network_guard`'s guard permanently on the xdist worker, breaking every later @allow_network test with \"Network access is forbidden\". This is exactly the mechanism that caused edeb; it is currently dormant because effect_spies was fixed, but nothing structurally prevents a new patcher from re-seeding it.\n\nThis is OPTIONAL defense-in-depth, not a fix for a live defect. Scope is test-only.\n\nAcceptance criteria (all observable, test-only):\n1. `_network_guard` teardown restores the real socket.connect/connect_ex regardless of intervening non-LIFO patching (self-healing: it captures and restores the true original, not whatever is currently installed).\n2. A RED regression test reproduces the non-LIFO re-seed (a monkeypatch-style patch layered over the active guard) and asserts a later @allow_network path still reaches the real socket — RED before the change, GREEN after.\n3. No change to production (non-test) code.\n4. Full unit suite passes under xdist (no isolation regressions).\n\nEscalation: whether to invest in this now vs leave filed is an operator call (flagged). Design decision if the self-healing approach turns out to require changing the guard's public contract.","id":"95b8-2754-da0c-4556","parent_id":"","priority":4,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Defense-in-depth: make _network_guard self-healing against non-LIFO patchers"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788137541786008146,"uuid":"d69d804c-e59f-4822-94fc-81e3f28a5a66"}